## Migration Analysis

Your code uses three patterns that must change for .NET 8:

| Pattern Found | Location | Issue on .NET 8 |
|---|---|---|
| **Cancellable work loop** | `while (_running)` | Works but can't be force-stopped |
| **ThreadAbortException catch** | `catch` block with rollback | Never thrown — cleanup never runs |
| **Thread.ResetAbort** | Inside catch | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| **Thread.Abort** | `Stop()` method | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            bool itemStarted = false;
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                itemStarted = true;
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Replaces ThreadAbortException catch — cancellation requested
                if (itemStarted)
                    RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break; // exit loop (replaces ResetAbort + continue behavior)
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Give the thread time to finish gracefully
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));

        _cts.Dispose();
        _cts = null;
    }
}
```

## Key Changes Explained

1. **`Thread.Abort()` → `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()`** — signals the thread cooperatively instead of injecting an exception.

2. **`catch (ThreadAbortException)` → `catch (OperationCanceledException)`** — the `when` filter ensures you only catch cancellations you initiated.

3. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` → removed** — cooperative cancellation doesn't auto-rethrow. You choose to `break` or continue.

4. **`Thread.Sleep(2000)` → `Thread.Join(timeout)`** — actually waits for thread completion rather than just hoping.

5. **Token passed to `GetNextItem`** — critical for unblocking. If this method uses `BlockingCollection<T>`, use `.Take(token)`. If it uses I/O, pass the token to async overloads.

## Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---|---|
| **`GetNextItem()` blocks forever** | If it doesn't accept a `CancellationToken`, cancellation stalls. You *must* make blocking calls cancellation-aware. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timers/callbacks. Always dispose in `Stop()`. |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | Use `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` to avoid swallowing unrelated exceptions. |
| **Assuming cancellation is instant** | It only fires at the next checkpoint. Add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside `ProcessItem` if it's long-running. |

## If `GetNextItem` Can't Be Modified

If the blocking call is in code you don't control, move it to a child process and use `Process.Kill()` as a last resort — this is the only safe equivalent of the old `Thread.Abort` force-stop behavior.
